WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:03.189
So far, the UI that we've looked at

2
00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:06.030
has been pretty boring and also pretty static.

3
00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.349
We can introduce a lot of dynamism to our UI

4
00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:10.070
extremely easily by taking advantage

5
00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:14.380
of UMG's inbuilt animation authoring tools.

6
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:15.980
A widget animation is essentially

7
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.480
a named property of a user widget.

8
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.539
Unlike skeletal animations, which are a separate asset,

9
00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:23.500
a widget's animations are baked directly

10
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.059
into the user widget and can be accessed as a property.

11
00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:30.879
As such, they're given unique names upon creation.

12
00:00:30.879 --> 00:00:33.539
Each animation is composed of several tracks

13
00:00:33.539 --> 00:00:37.380
mirroring the pipeline used in Sequencer or Matinee.

14
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.579
Each track is typically linked to a widget,

15
00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:41.539
and from there we can add additional subtracks

16
00:00:41.539 --> 00:00:44.250
under that first widget-based track.

17
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.229
These subtracks are responsible for manipulating

18
00:00:46.229 --> 00:00:49.829
some property of its associated widget.

19
00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:51.350
So in this exact instance,

20
00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.750
we have an animation called move.

21
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:55.750
Inside the move animation, we have tracks

22
00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:59.509
all linked to an image, a border, and a spacer.

23
00:00:59.509 --> 00:01:01.509
Each of these subtracks have got their properties

24
00:01:01.549 --> 00:01:03.549
keyframed independently of each other,

25
00:01:03.549 --> 00:01:05.590
making quite complex animations possible

26
00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:07.969
with very simple modifications.

27
00:01:07.969 --> 00:01:10.750
Here, we're manipulating the transform, pivots,

28
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.129
and opacities, but any value that we can change

29
00:01:13.129 --> 00:01:14.810
over time from a gameplay point of view

30
00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.439
can realistically be manipulated through a keyframe track.

31
00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:21.159
It's worth noting that the values of particular keyframes

32
00:01:21.159 --> 00:01:23.040
can't be changed at runtime.

33
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.120
We'll see this more in depth

34
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.680
with the live example at the end of the talk,

35
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.400
but the long and short of it is

36
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.239
widget animations are for static changes.

37
00:01:30.239 --> 00:01:32.000
For example, we can't have an animation blend

38
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.640
from one color to another

39
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.760
where that color is chosen at runtime.

40
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.480
We'll need to bake this animation into the widget

41
00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.750
during the authoring phase.

42
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:42.810
So let's talk through creating a very simple animation

43
00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:45.329
using the star image from the previous slide.

44
00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:46.689
As you can see, it's nothing fancy.

45
00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:47.810
We're just going to be

46
00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:49.930
manipulating its transform property.

47
00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.349
However, the principles here can apply more universally

48
00:01:52.349 --> 00:01:56.310
to create a much more complex animation down the line.

49
00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:57.310
First things first,

50
00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.049
we need to come down to the animation section

51
00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:00.670
at the bottom of the UMG editor

52
00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:02.549
and hit the green animation button.

53
00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:04.469
This will create a new widget animation

54
00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:06.430
embedding it in this user widget.

55
00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:09.719
We'll name it move as an example.

56
00:02:09.719 --> 00:02:11.639
Next, with the animation that we want to work with

57
00:02:11.639 --> 00:02:13.319
selected, we'll select the widget

58
00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:15.039
we want to add a track for.

59
00:02:15.039 --> 00:02:17.780
In this case, we want to animate our star image.

60
00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.680
Once that's selected, we can hit the track button

61
00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.439
and select a track that handles the property

62
00:02:21.439 --> 00:02:22.759
we want to animate.

63
00:02:22.759 --> 00:02:25.199
In this instance, we'll just hit the transform button.

64
00:02:25.199 --> 00:02:26.900
From here, we can keyframe any properties

65
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.240
within the transform that we might want to manipulate.

66
00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.080
For this example, we're adding a keyframe

67
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:32.860
to its translation at zero,

68
00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.560
moving its X value by just over a thousand pixels

69
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.000
at the one second mark,

70
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.000
and then moving it back to zero a second later,

71
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.500
creating the example on the opening slide.

72
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:42.340
And that's it.

73
00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:43.620
We can now bind this animation

74
00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.979
using the bind widget anim U property specifier

75
00:02:45.979 --> 00:02:47.780
that we'll be touching on in just a second.

76
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.000
We'll play it outright in the blueprint graph.

77
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.139
This animation is now a member

78
00:02:51.139 --> 00:02:52.300
of the user widget after all,

79
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:53.620
so there's nothing stopping us

80
00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:55.060
from grabbing it in the blueprint backend

81
00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.939
and simply calling play.

82
00:02:56.939 --> 00:02:58.099
Now all the visual stuff's out of the way,

83
00:02:58.099 --> 00:03:00.800
let's take a look at some of the code that drives it.

84
00:03:01.319 --> 00:03:03.719
We won't be covering the under the hood slate magic.

85
00:03:03.719 --> 00:03:05.159
We'll be touching on how we can leverage

86
00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:06.719
the existing tool set Unreal offers

87
00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.560
to author smart UI elements,

88
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:09.580
and how we could integrate them

89
00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:11.719
with our gameplay systems on a code level.
